////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Acid Test: Auto Constants 2 - the invisible structural heads
//
// The invisible structural heads (maps, index, tuple, set, setbuilder,
// class, trans_chain - parsers/notation-tables.js invisibleHeads, Ken
// approved 2026-08-03) are auto-declared constants: the user types
// `f : A to B` or `⟨a,b⟩` and never the head words themselves, so "what
// constant should I Declare?" is unanswerable for them, and a Rule using
// such notation with its head left a metavariable can instantiate the
// head to a DIFFERENT invisible head of the same arity - the same
// soundness hazard class the big-operator heads closed (sum := defint,
// see Auto Constants.lurch).
//
// The motivating hazard here: `f : A to B` parses to (maps f A B) and
// `⟨g,X,Y⟩` to (tuple g X Y), both ternary applications, so with maps a
// metavariable the domain rule below instantiates maps := tuple inside
// its equation weeny and a 3-tuple falsely justifies a domain claim.
// (The equation shape is just one instance: a bare metavariable-headed
// premise fires the same way - `:{ :Maps(f,A,B) Maps(f,B,A) }` with
// undeclared capital-M Maps lets Likes(food,friends,meatballs) justify
// Likes(food,meatballs,friends), verified 2026-08-03 - which for user
// symbols like Maps is intended second-order Rule behavior, and for the
// invisible heads is exactly what auto-declaration prevents.)
//
// Each section below uses its own disjoint constants so that rules and
// instantiations cannot cross-justify between sections.  The head maps
// is deliberately NOT declared ANYWHERE in this file - interpretation
// hoists every Declare to the top of the document, so a Declare in any
// section would neutralize the hazard - because auto-declaration is
// exactly what is under test (the third section redeclares OTHER
// invisible heads for the harmlessness check).

Declare =, dom

➤ "an undeclared maps head is not a metavariable: a 3-tuple must not instantiate it"
{
  Declare g, X, Y
  Rule: :{ dom((f : A to B)) = A }
  dom(⟨g,X,Y⟩) = X ✗
}

➤ "the same rule still fires on real maps statements: f, A, B stay metavariables"
{
  Declare h, U, V
  Rule: :{ dom((f : A to B)) = A }
  dom((h : U to V)) = U ✔︎
}

➤ "re-declaring invisible heads is harmless"
{
  Declare tuple, index, j, S, T
  Rule: :{ dom((f : A to B)) = A }
  dom((j : S to T)) = S ✔︎
}
